{"author":"Robert Jamieson, A. R. Fausset and David Brown","book":"Commentary Critical and Explanatory on the Whole Bible","chapter":602,"classification":{"bucket":"Christian Classics / Commentaries","canonicalStatus":"noncanonical","contentKind":"commentary","cautionReason":"","doctrinalStatus":"historical","labels":["christian-classics","commentaries"],"severity":0},"id":"classics:jfb:Ps.124","lineage":[],"metadata":{"author":"Robert Jamieson, A. R. Fausset and David Brown","authorshipDateRange":"19th century","genre":"Commentaries","tradition":"Protestant","translator":null,"edition":"1871 one-volume text; expanded electronic edition by Ernie Stefanik","rights":"Public Domain","source":{"id":"jfb","provider":"CCEL","sourceUrl":"https://www.ccel.org/ccel/jamieson/jfb.html","title":"Commentary Critical and Explanatory on the Whole Bible"},"searchIndexed":false},"originalBook":"Psalms","sourceVolumeId":"jfb","source":{"id":"jfb","sourceUrl":"https://www.ccel.org/ccel/jamieson/jfb.html","title":"Commentary Critical and Explanatory on the Whole Bible"},"title":"Psalms 124","reference":"Psalms 124","notes":[],"verses":[{"verse":1,"text":"PSALM 124","sourceParagraph":1},{"verse":2,"text":"Ps 124:1-8.","sourceParagraph":2,"scriptureVerseStart":1,"scriptureVerseEnd":1},{"verse":3,"text":"The writer, for the Church, praises God for past, and expresses trust for future, deliverance from foes.","sourceParagraph":2,"scriptureVerseStart":1,"scriptureVerseEnd":1},{"verse":4,"text":"1, 2. on our side—for us (Ps 56:9).","sourceParagraph":3,"scriptureVerseStart":1,"scriptureVerseEnd":1},{"verse":5,"text":"now—or, \"oh! let Israel\"","sourceParagraph":4,"scriptureVerseStart":1,"scriptureVerseEnd":1},{"verse":6,"text":"2. rose … against, &c.—(Ps 3:1; 56:11).","sourceParagraph":5,"scriptureVerseStart":2,"scriptureVerseEnd":2},{"verse":7,"text":"3.","sourceParagraph":6,"scriptureVerseStart":3,"scriptureVerseEnd":3},{"verse":8,"text":"Then—that is, the time of our danger.","sourceParagraph":6,"scriptureVerseStart":3,"scriptureVerseEnd":3},{"verse":9,"text":"quick—literally, \"living\" (Nu 16:32, 33), description of ferocity.","sourceParagraph":7,"scriptureVerseStart":3,"scriptureVerseEnd":3},{"verse":10,"text":"4, 5.","sourceParagraph":8,"scriptureVerseStart":4,"scriptureVerseEnd":4},{"verse":11,"text":"(Compare Ps 18:4, 16).","sourceParagraph":8,"scriptureVerseStart":4,"scriptureVerseEnd":4},{"verse":12,"text":"5.","sourceParagraph":9,"scriptureVerseStart":5,"scriptureVerseEnd":5},{"verse":13,"text":"The epithet proud added to waters denotes insolent enemies.","sourceParagraph":9,"scriptureVerseStart":5,"scriptureVerseEnd":5},{"verse":14,"text":"6, 7.","sourceParagraph":10,"scriptureVerseStart":6,"scriptureVerseEnd":6},{"verse":15,"text":"The figure is changed to that of a rapacious wild beast (Ps 3:7), and then of a fowler (Ps 91:3), and complete escape is denoted by breaking the net.","sourceParagraph":10,"scriptureVerseStart":6,"scriptureVerseEnd":6},{"verse":16,"text":"8.","sourceParagraph":11,"scriptureVerseStart":8,"scriptureVerseEnd":8},{"verse":17,"text":"(Compare Ps 121:2).","sourceParagraph":11,"scriptureVerseStart":8,"scriptureVerseEnd":8},{"verse":18,"text":"name—in the usual sense (Ps 5:11; 20:1).","sourceParagraph":12,"scriptureVerseStart":8,"scriptureVerseEnd":8},{"verse":19,"text":"He thus places over against the great danger the omnipotent God, and drowns, as it were in an anthem, the wickedness of the whole world and of hell, just as a great fire consumes a little drop of water [Luther].","sourceParagraph":12,"scriptureVerseStart":8,"scriptureVerseEnd":8}],"scriptureReference":{"book":"Psalms","chapter":124}}